1. Protocols & UsesHTTP: Full form: Hypertext Transfer Protocol; used to transfer unencrypted web web page data between a web browser and a server.HTTPS: Full form: Hypertext Transfer Protocol Secure; used to securely transfer web page data by encrypting communication via TLS/SSL.FTP: Full form: File Transfer Protocol; used to transfer and manage files between a client computer and a network server.SMTP: Full form: Simple Mail Transfer Protocol; used to send and route outgoing email messages across networks.2. IPv4 vs. IPv6IPv4 uses a 32-bit numerical address format (e.g., 192.168.1.1), providing around 4.3 billion unique addresses.IPv6 uses a 128-bit hexadecimal address format (e.g., 2001:db8::ff00:42), offering an almost unlimited pool of unique addresses to replace the exhausted IPv4 pool.3. Running nslookup google.comRunning nslookup google.com queries DNS servers for Google's current IP addresses. Because Google uses global load balancers, the exact IP address varies depending on location and network.  Sample IPv4 Address: 142.250.190.46 (or common Google DNS 8.8.8.8)Sample IPv6 Address: 2607:f8b0:4004:837::200e4. HTTP Status Codes200 (OK): The request was successful, and the server returned the requested data.301 (Moved Permanently): The requested resource has been permanently moved to a new URL.404 (Not Found): The requested server was reached, but the specific page or resource could not be found.500 (Internal Server Error): The server encountered an unexpected condition that prevented it from fulfilling the request.403 (Forbidden): The server understood the request, but the client does not have permission to access the resource.5. DNS Hierarchy Explained ([www.google.com](https://www.google.com))Root Zone (.): The top level of the DNS hierarchy (represented by an implicit dot at the end). It directs queries to the appropriate TLD server.TLD - Top-Level Domain (.com): Managed by TLD registry servers, it handles all domains ending in .com and points to Google's specific domain name servers.Domain (google): The second-level domain (google.com) owned by Google, registered under the .com TLD.Subdomain (www): A specific sub-section or hostname created under google.com to direct traffic specifically to its main web service.
